<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import LedgerFrame from './lib/LedgerFrame.abide'
import ledgerFrameSource from './lib/LedgerFrame.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import layoutSource from './frame/layout.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import contactsSource from './frame/contacts.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import invoicesSource from './frame/invoices.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A layout is a component with a slot">
        <Code language="abide" caption="src/layout.abide — excerpt" code={`<script>
import { principal } from '@abide/abide'
</script>

<header>
    <a href="/">Ledger</a>
    <span>{principal.resolved.name}</span>
</header>

<main><slot/></main>`} />

        <p><code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code> is where the child page renders, and it is the same
        <code>&lt;slot/&gt;</code> any component has. There is no layout API and no second kind of
        component: a layout is the component the router puts a page inside.</p>

        <p>Layouts <strong>nest</strong> by route. A <code>layout.abide</code> at
        <code>src/</code> and another at <code>src/invoices/</code> means an invoice page renders
        inside both, outermost first, which is how a section gets its own sidebar without the app's
        header being mentioned twice.</p>

        <p>A layout's <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> files hooks over the same routes, so
        <code>middleware</code> in <code>src/admin/layout.abide</code> guards every admin page
        and handler. One with no markup wraps nothing and is that section's hooks alone.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/reuse-a-piece-of-markup')}>Components</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What a layout buys is what it does not rebuild">
        <Example
            id="layout-kept"
            title="`layout.abide` — the header two pages share, built once"
            summary="A ledger whose header holds a note for the call you are on. Type one, then move between contacts and invoices: the page in the slot changes, and the note is still there."
            files={{
                'frame/layout.abide': layoutSource,
                'frame/contacts.abide': contactsSource,
                'frame/invoices.abide': invoicesSource,
                'lib/LedgerFrame.abide': ledgerFrameSource,
            }}
        >
            <LedgerFrame />
        </Example>

        <p>The reason to put chrome in a layout rather than in each page is not that it saves
        typing. It is that a shared layout is <strong>not rebuilt</strong> across a navigation: the
        header keeps its scroll position, an open menu stays open, and a component in the sidebar
        that was mid-load is still mid-load.</p>

        <p>A page that renders its own header gets none of that, and the symptom is subtle —
        everything looks right, and a menu closes itself every time somebody follows a link.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a> ·
        <a href={url('/pages/animate-from-one-page-to-the-next')}>View transitions</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A layout reads the route it wraps">
        <p><code>route.params</code> and <code>route.name</code> are ambient, so a layout reads them
        without a page passing anything down. That is what a breadcrumb wants, and what a sidebar
        highlighting the current section wants.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/add-a-page')}>Routes</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> — a section's
            rungs, in its layout</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/add-a-page')}>Routes</a> — the file that becomes the page in
            the slot</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/set-the-title-and-social-preview')}>Head &amp; metadata</a> —
            what a layout contributes to the head</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/show-a-page-when-something-fails')}>Error pages</a> — resolved
            the same nearest-ancestor way</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
